From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 12:23:15 -0500
Subject: fix: the lit circle is the size of the chips beside it

At 20 DIP the circle behind a lit shield or moon was right in strength and a
little small. It is 21 DIP now, the size of the address field's chips on the
same row. An odd size has edges between pixels at 1x, so the bounds are a
RectF: the enclosing Rect would have painted 22.

Why: the owner's look on the Mac, 2026-11-06 (BACKLOG S-423, toolbar T60).

The same files carry two later rules for the row's page colour. A page that
dims itself under its own dialog leaves the row its colour (toolbar T62,
BACKLOG S-468, GitHub issue 101). And over a top edge of several colours, a
page's declared colour -- its theme-color, else its background -- colours
the row only when it shows along a third of that edge or more; otherwise the
row is the window's own, and leaves a colour it had (toolbar T63, BACKLOG
S-487). start.me declares a bright blue, #0093d5, over a start page whose top
is a dark photograph, and the row stayed that blue (the owner, 2026-11-14).

Removable when: fold it into the patch that made the circle, once the Mac's
series matches this one; T62 and T63 belong in the patch that reads the
page's top edge (toolbar T31).
